  "entries": {
    "buoy": {
      "word": "BUOY",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "A float; esp. a floating object moored to the bottom, to mark a channel or to point out the position of something beneath the water, as an anchor, shoal, rock, etc. Anchor buoy, a buoy attached to, or marking the position of, an anchor. the motion of the waves. anchorage. conical or pear-shaped. into the water, until a boat can be dispatched to save them. to a point at each end. side into the water, before letting go the anchor. action of the waves.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To keep from sinking in a fluid, as in water or air; to keep afloat; -- with up.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To support or sustain; to preserve from sinking into ruin or despondency. Those old prejudices, which buoy up the ponderous mass of his nobility, wealth, and title. Burke.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To fix buoys to; to mark by a buoy or by buoys; as, to buoy an anchor; to buoy or buoy off a channel. Not one rock near the surface was discovered which was not buoyed by this floating weed. Darwin.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To float; to rise like a buoy. \"Rising merit will buoy up at last.\" Pope.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
